  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1242 AM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nNot much has changed with the upcoming weather system that will \naffect the area today through Friday. A closed low over the northern \nPlains will merge with a shortwave trough over the southern Plains, \nleading to a large closed low that will track over the area through \nThanksgiving, exiting the region on Friday. Winds are still expected \nto be the main impact with this system, mainly in the mountains and \nfoothills from this afternoon through Thursday morning. \n\nAs low level cyclogenesis occurs near the GA coast later today, a \ntight pressure gradient will develop along the Appalachians. \nEasterly 850 mb winds pick up to around 50 kt, with the RRFS showing \na few spots around 60 kt. This is not a typical mountain wave setup \nhowever, as winds are coming directly from the east, maybe slightly \nnorth of east. This adds a layer of uncertainty to which areas will \nhave high winds, and how high those winds may be enhanced by local \nterrain. The current Advisory wording seems to be the best way to \nhandle it - gusts up to 55 mph, with a few peaks potentially seeing \ngusts over 60 mph.  Our SW NC counties and eastern Monroe will be \nadded to the Wind Advisory based on the latest REFS probabilities \nover 50% of wind gusts reaching 40+ in the higher elevations of \nthose counties. This will also better align with the Advisory in N \nGA. \n\nRain amounts for this event will be impacted by the downslope flow \nin the eastern sections, and areas west of the I-81/I-40/I-75 \ncorridor will likely see the highest rain amounts in the area, \nprobably 3/4 of an inch to a little over 1 inch. Rainfall is \nexpected to increase this afternoon and evening and continue through \nmuch of Thanksgiving Day, though it will be trending lighter \nThursday afternoon. Some wrap around moisture on the backside of the \nsystem could promote a few flakes falling, particularly in the \nmountains, Thursday night into Friday morning. Only light \naccumulations are expected at this time, with only some higher \nelevations seeing up to 1 inch. Surface temperatures suggest mainly \nlight rain or drizzle for any northern valley locations that see \nlingering light precip Friday.\n\nValley locations are also expected to see some gusty winds from \nThursday afternoon through Friday as the surface low deepens and \nhigh pressure builds in from the west. 20 to 30 mph gusts seem \nlikely, but some locations could gust near 35 mph as SW winds get \nchanneled up the TN Valley. Valley locations should remain below \nadvisory criteria for Thursday and Friday. \n\nThis system will finally eject from the region Friday with sunshine \nand dry weather returning Saturday. This will be short-lived, \nhowever, as an additional trough and cold front bring more rain \nchances late Sunday/early Monday. Thursday and Friday will feature \ncooler temperatures with the weekend returning closer to seasonal \nnorms.",
